# CP17.1 — chart 1, then commit.
#
# CP17 measured HYBRID and could not draw it. Neither `settle/eval/charts.py`
# nor `out/charts/` was on its allowlist, and the omission was not cosmetic:
# `charts.py` draws every arm in `metrics.json`, so the moment HYBRID entered
# that file the committed PNG went stale and CHT-1 failed. This closes it.

settle/eval/charts.py
out/charts/
README.md
KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md
viewer/index.html
SPEC.md
PLAN.md
checkpoints/cp17_1.allowlist

# --- F38: marked, not merely present -----------------------------------------
#
# HYBRID gets its own colour, a diamond marker, and a COMPOSED label naming what
# it is made of — the treatment B3 gets for OBSERVE, for the same reason. An
# unmarked point on a recovery-against-contacts chart reads as a rival policy,
# and HYBRID is a measurement of class-based routing that is explicitly not the
# submitted policy. A chart that let that pass unqualified would be doing the
# thing the per-class breakdown exists to prevent.
#
# The four crowded points are placed by region rather than by nudged offsets.
# Two rounds of nudging produced a chart with B3's label written over the title
# and HYBRID's running off the right edge; a placement map fixed it in one. The
# caption moved to `figure.text` in a reserved margin, because at four lines it
# collided with the x-label whatever axes offset it was given.

# --- F40: stated as a choice, with the price attached -------------------------
#
# The Known Limitations entry says OURS forgoes 3.5 points of recovery — Rs
# 227,318 on this batch — deliberately, and that a merchant valuing recovered
# rupees above customer contact should prefer HYBRID. The table there is the
# case FOR the alternative rather than against it.
#
# That is the honest shape. Our ordering is a product judgement the evidence
# does not force: it rests on holding that 285 opt-outs and 50 people chased
# after they had already paid outweigh Rs 227,318, and a merchant with different
# churn economics can reasonably weigh it the other way. What the section
# refuses to do is present 28.37% as the best available number. It is the best
# number available under a constraint we chose, and the unconstrained figure is
# 31.87%.

# --- What is deliberately NOT here --------------------------------------------
#
# The viewer's per-class table still compares OURS against B2 only. Adding a
# HYBRID column needs `_class_rows` in `settle/eval/report.py`, which is not on
# this allowlist, and F41 asked for the batch table — which has HYBRID marked
# COMPOSED and now carries an opt-outs-induced row, the number that makes the
# tradeoff legible at a glance.
#
# The per-class data is in `out/viewer_data.json` under `by_decline_class`
# already, so the column is a rendering change whenever report.py next opens.
